(PRC-77 — undocumented precision, and a hazard: the pid expression reaches
 kill(2) unfiltered, so the two broadcast pids are reachable from a Vox
 program. pid 0 signals the caller's whole PROCESS GROUP; pid -1 signals
 every process the uid is allowed to signal. LANGUAGE.md:4156-4157
 documents exactly this lowering ("<pid-expr> is the target PID (loaded
 into rdi)") without noting that two ordinary-looking integers are
 broadcasts.
 SAFETY: this probe sends signal 0 and ONLY signal 0. The kernel delivers
 nothing for signal 0; it performs the permission check and returns. Both
 calls returning cleanly is the finding — with any real signal number
 those same two lines would have hit every process this user owns,
 including the fuzzer and this session.
 Minimal repro at D5.vox.
 Ran, from the repo root:
   VOX_CORE_PATH=/home/josj/scr/english/vox/coreasm \
   /home/josj/scr/english/vox/target/release/vox \
   docs/ledger/probes/process-control/PRC-77.vox -o p && ./p
 expected output:
   pid 0 reached kill(2) and returned success
   pid -1 reached kill(2) and returned success
)
Send signal 0 to process 0.
On error print "pid 0 was rejected".
print "pid 0 reached kill(2) and returned success".
Send signal 0 to process -1.
On error print "pid -1 was rejected".
print "pid -1 reached kill(2) and returned success".
